      "summaries": {
        "en": "Fastfetch is a command-line system information tool that displays detailed hardware and software configurations quickly, addressing the need for efficient system diagnostics without graphical interfaces. It achieves exceptional speed through optimized C code and minimal dependencies, making it significantly faster than similar tools like Neofetch. Developers can use it for system monitoring scripts, customize output formats via configuration files, or extend it by adding new modules for specific hardware detection.",
        "zh": "Fastfetch是一款命令行系统信息工具，能够快速显示详细的硬件和软件配置信息，解决了在无图形界面环境下高效诊断系统状态的需求。它通过优化的C语言代码和最小化依赖实现卓越性能，比Neofetch等同类工具运行速度更快。用户可通过配置文件自定义输出格式，将其集成到系统监控脚本中，或通过添加新模块来扩展特定硬件检测功能，适合开发者和系统管理员进行日常系统维护和性能分析。",
        "es": "Fastfetch is a command-line system information tool that displays detailed hardware and software configurations quickly, addressing the need for efficient system diagnostics without graphical interfaces. It achieves exceptional speed through optimized C code and minimal dependencies, making it significantly faster than similar tools like Neofetch. Developers can use it for system monitoring scripts, customize output formats via configuration files, or extend it by adding new modules for specific hardware detection.",
        "sections": {
          "why": "fastfetch stands out for bsdfetch, command-line, and fastfetch in real-world workflows.",
          "what": "Fastfetch is a command-line system information tool that displays detailed hardware and software configurations quickly, addressing the need for efficient system diagnostics without graphical interfaces. It achieves exceptional speed through optimized C code and minimal dependencies, making it significantly faster than similar tools like Neofetch. Developers can use it for system monitoring scripts, customize output formats via configuration files, or extend it by adding new modules for specific hardware detection.",
          "how": "Review https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch for docs, run starter examples, and integrate with your pipeline or evaluation stack."
        }
      },
